t i've experienced  since i left ea and a real kind of team  and i'm honestly super grateful for all  of you i think streaming is something  that i'm definitely gonna do a lot more  of  in the future so throughout these past  few weeks and  all of this thinking about what's going  on and  the done is better than perfect but on  the other hand getting stuff  done to a higher degree and to a higher  level of satisfaction really is also  something that i think is important what  i've decided to do  is to take a little bit of a step back  from hazel and  instead of trying to get all of the  features in at once which is something  that i tend to do and then later i just  get overwhelmed  i'm going to take a look at what we  already have because i think that hazel  dev  is at a really good place right now i  mean you could basically make a game  with it it's got c-sharp scripting  it's got 3d rendering it's got 2d  physics you can kind of put something  together with it and in fact that's  something that i am going to do soon on  my stream i want to start building games  building little games  using hazel using the actual editor  using actual c-sharp scripting  without actually going in and  programming any c plus plus code that's  something that i'm really excited  to do in the future and obviously i'll  have to add features to the c  plus plus side of things to facilitate a  lot of the  functionality that's probably missing  and that i would like to add along the  way  but that's also part of the fun and  that's how we get to a point where hazel  ends up being more feature complete  so taking a look at what hazel dev  already has  and then just refining that that's  something that i'm going to spend  the next month or two going over i am  going to go over the renderer and refine  it  i'm going to be integrating things like  spur v which is a shader compiler which  we can also use to compile into various  other shading languages as well as  reflective on the shaders to get  information about things like uniform  buffers and  vertex attribute layouts because the way  that we're currently doing that is just  by using a simple glsl parser that i  wrote that's also going to set us  up for multiple rendering apis and for  multiple platforms which is also  something that i'm going to be taking a  look at  in the next month or two i'm going to be  taking a look seriously into mac os and  linux support  and everything that we need to do to  make that happen i'm going to spend a  lot of time polishing what hazel  actually has and lifting that up to a  higher degree of quality just to a  higher standard  instead of trying to jam in a whole  bunch of new features and to be honest  i'm really excited to do that because in  a sense that's a lot easier  than trying to learn all these new  things and deal with problems you've  never dealt with before  it's time to just get back to this and  just improve the overall  quality of it and i honestly can't wait  to do that now apart from the fact that  i will be keeping you guys up to date in  these hateful devlogs and hopefully next  time i'll try some code and  all the the actual ways that i've  improved the code i guess  apart from that i am also going to be  streaming myself doing a lot of this  work now  in the past i used to stream myself  working on hazel dev only for patreons  but  i'm not going to be doing that anymore  i'm going to be streaming for everyone  on twitch over the past week of  streaming on tuition general i found  that apart from the fact that it's super  cool  to have an audience watch you and  comment on what you're doing and it just  it just  motivates me to keep going more because  i know there's people watching me  apart from all of that a lot of you guys  are excellent at spotting mistakes or  just  bouncing ideas off of sometimes i'll not  know how to do something or i'll  consider doing it a certain way and i'll  be thinking out loud and just talking to  you and  and som